Seeing that, I would back away quietly! I'm not sure that having the R 
Python interface try to delete files is a good idea!

Please try out Rpy on very simple examples first to see how the
representations work. Say try two numeric vectors from rnorm(), and try a
t.test(). See if you can access the components in the htest object
returned. My guess would be, without ever having tried Rpy seriously, that
the arguments to rast.get() are not being passed correctly. Is there any
way to run R debug from Rpy (probably not)? Could you try to write your
own function to source into R from a file, that you know works without
Rpy, and that could avoid having to move anything but simple objects
across the R/Python interface - just single character strings and integer
or numeric vectors?

